import json,pathlib
modules=[];cards=[];questions=[];cases=[]
def module(mid,title,subtitle,pages,lesson,rows):
    prereq=[modules[-1]['id']] if modules else []
    modules.append(dict(id=mid,title=title,subtitle=subtitle,pages=pages,prerequisites=prereq,lesson=lesson))
    for i,line in enumerate(rows.strip().splitlines(),1):
        a=line.split('|')
        assert len(a)==9,(mid,i,len(a))
        front,back,prompt,correct,why,w1,e1,w2,e2=a
        cid=f'{mid}-{i:02}'
        cards.append(dict(id=cid,module=mid,front=front,back=back,difficulty='Foundation' if i<=3 else 'Applied' if i<=7 else 'Advanced',sources=[dict(id='guide',pages=pages)],prerequisites=prereq))
        options=[dict(text=correct,explanation=why),dict(text=w1,explanation=e1),dict(text=w2,explanation=e2)]
        # rotate authored options so position is not a clue; stable IDs survive sessions
        shift=(len(questions)*7+1)%3
        options=options[shift:]+options[:shift]
        questions.append(dict(id='q-'+cid,module=mid,prompt=prompt,options=options,answer=(3-shift)%3,difficulty=cards[-1]['difficulty'],sources=[dict(id='guide',pages=pages)],prerequisites=prereq))
module('foundations','The five views of a loan','Understand the contract before the calculator',[3,4,35],[
'A loan joins a borrower promise to a lender asset. Amount, rate, tenor and repayment frequency describe the starting contract; fees, tranches, moratoriums and later amendments make its servicing behavior specific. A sanctioned amount is an approved limit. A confirmed disbursement creates funded exposure. They can differ.',
'Inspect every event through five views: business instruction, bank cash, contractual schedule, accounting movement, and control evidence. An on-time EMI is simple only when all five agree. A bank credit can be real while its borrower is unknown; a schedule can show a due amount while no cash has arrived.',
'Principal is unpaid funded exposure. Interest compensates time and risk; it is a separate component. A reducing-balance loan earns interest on the agreed outstanding basis. A flat-rate quotation uses original principal and does not describe the same yield as a reducing rate.',
'An overdraft needs utilization and limits; an EMI term loan needs dated installments. A tranche loan needs interest on confirmed drawdowns. Product choice therefore changes the model, rather than merely changing the screen label.',
'Worked example: ₹3,00,000 sanctioned, ₹1,20,000 disbursed, ₹20,000 principal repaid. Funded principal outstanding is ₹1,00,000, with ₹1,80,000 undisbursed sanction. Future interest is not part of principal. These quantities answer different questions.'
],'''
What are the five views of a loan event?|Business, cash, schedule, accounting and control evidence must explain the same event.|A bank statement shows ₹12,000 credited but the borrower is unknown. Which view is already confirmed?|Cash movement|The bank credit confirms cash, while identification and allocation remain unresolved.|Loan closure|A bank credit alone does not clear all obligations or closure tasks.|Interest income|A receipt is not necessarily earned interest.
Sanction versus disbursement: what creates funded exposure?|Confirmed disbursement creates funded principal; sanction records approved lending capacity.|₹5,00,000 is sanctioned and only ₹2,00,000 paid out. Before any repayment, funded principal is what?|₹2,00,000|Only confirmed disbursements create funded principal in this example.|₹5,00,000|This is the sanction limit, not the amount funded.|₹3,00,000|This is the remaining undisbursed sanction.
How do principal and interest differ?|Principal tracks the unpaid amount funded. Interest is separately earned under time and rate conventions.|A ₹10,000 receipt clears ₹2,000 interest and ₹8,000 principal. How much does POS reduce?|₹8,000|Only the principal allocation reduces principal outstanding.|₹10,000|The interest portion clears a different component.|₹2,000|This is interest paid, not principal repaid.
Why can two 12% rate quotes have different costs?|Flat and reducing conventions use different interest bases, and fees and timing affect cashflow yield.|Two lenders quote 12%; one is flat and one reducing. What is needed for a fair cost comparison?|Dated borrower cashflows including disclosed costs|Cashflows expose differences in basis, fees and timing.|The rate labels alone|The labels do not identify the interest basis or costs.|Only the sanctioned amount|Sanction does not capture repayment cost.
What determines whether early cash is a prepayment?|Borrower instruction, contract and applicable policy determine advance versus principal treatment.|With no dues, a borrower pays ₹15,000 explicitly for future EMI under an advance policy. What follows?|Hold an advance liability until application|The stated instruction calls for future application rather than immediate principal curtailment.|Reduce POS immediately in every case|This contradicts the specified advance policy.|Recognize all as interest income|Future interest has not yet been earned.
How does an overdraft differ from an EMI term loan?|An overdraft tracks utilization, drawing limits and daily interest rather than a fixed amortization table alone.|A revolving facility has a ₹4,00,000 limit and ₹70,000 utilization. What drives the specified daily-interest base?|₹70,000 utilization|Interest in this example is based on drawn funds.|₹4,00,000 limit|Unused lending capacity is not drawn principal.|The previous term-loan EMI|An unrelated installment does not describe this facility.
What does a principal-only moratorium defer?|It defers principal obligations; interest treatment must be read separately.|A contract defers principal for two months but requires monthly interest payments. Which expectation is correct?|Interest remains payable as stated|Only principal is deferred in the given contract.|All interest is waived|Deferral of principal is not waiver of interest.|Principal is written off|A timing change does not extinguish principal.
Why is closure a workflow?|Financial balances, cash, customer documents and operational obligations need separate controlled completion.|All financial dues are cleared but collateral release is pending. Which status best explains the situation?|Financial closure complete; operational release pending|Separate states make the remaining task visible.|Everything complete with no pending work|The release task still exists.|Borrower payment failed|The prompt says the dues are cleared.
Why are funded exposure and book carrying amount different?|Carrying amount can reflect fee amortization and impairment; funded exposure measures principal rights.|Gross carrying amount is ₹1,00,000 and allowance ₹5,000, with no other adjustments. Net carrying amount is what?|₹95,000|Net equals gross carrying amount less allowance.|₹1,05,000|Allowance reduces rather than increases the net asset.|₹5,000|This is the allowance, not the net asset.
What evidence makes a balance explainable?|Trace source events, component movements, rule versions and effective dates to the displayed balance.|Support sees an unexplained POS change. What is the strongest first investigation?|Trace principal movements to source events and approvals|Event-linked movements show why the exposure changed.|Replace POS with the schedule estimate|An estimate can conceal the operational defect.|Delete old schedule versions|That removes evidence needed to explain the change.
''')
module('boundaries','LOS, LMS, payments and GL','Know who owns each state transition',[5,6,28,29],[
'LOS usually manages application, underwriting, sanction and accepted terms. LMS usually manages the booked contract after disbursement: schedules, bills, receipts, allocations and servicing. CBS and specialist systems may divide these duties differently; document an explicit owner for each fact.',
'A controlled handoff validates final accepted terms, borrower identity references, product version, fees, rate, tenor, mandates and partner terms. Store the accepted version rather than silently applying the newest product defaults to an older offer.',
'Payment instructions and bank confirmations answer whether money was requested or moved. Allocation answers which loan components were cleared. Accounting records debit and credit movements; GL aggregates those movements into control accounts. One webhook should not invent all of these facts.',
'The monetary transaction boundary should keep allocation, loan balances and accounting event creation consistent. Downstream notifications and reporting can use a durable outbox and retry. Eventual consistency still needs completion evidence and exception ownership.',
'Worked trace: bank credit → reconciled receipt → borrower identification → versioned allocation → balanced sub-ledger journal → GL export acknowledgment. A failed GL export should be retried using the existing batch identity, rather than collecting the customer payment again.'
],'''
What does LOS normally own?|Application, underwriting, sanction and final offer acceptance before controlled booking.|A bureau-based underwriting rule changes before sanction. Which domain normally owns this decision?|LOS / credit decisioning|The change concerns origination rather than post-disbursement servicing.|Escrow settlement ledger|Escrow records cash routing, not underwriting.|GL export service|The GL aggregates accounting movements.
What does LMS normally own?|Booked loan servicing: schedules, dues, allocations, balances and servicing mutations.|A borrower asks to reduce EMI after a valid part-prepayment. Which domain normally produces the new servicing schedule?|LMS schedule engine|It manages post-disbursement schedule mutations.|Lead CRM|Lead capture does not own the booked schedule.|Bank statement parser alone|A parser observes money but cannot interpret contractual amendments.
What makes booking handoff controlled?|Validate and preserve the final accepted terms, identities, product and disclosure versions.|LOS accepted 11% but product defaults now say 12%. What should booking do?|Validate against the accepted terms and record any authorized amendment|An updated default cannot silently replace the accepted offer.|Book at 12% without evidence|This silently changes agreed terms.|Discard the accepted offer|It removes the basis for validation.
What is a system of record?|The defined authoritative owner of a fact, with controlled changes and reconciliation to other views.|LMS and CBS show different POS. What should be defined before a repair?|Authoritative ownership and reconciled event mapping|The team needs to know which facts and movements each system owns.|Whichever number is smaller always wins|Size does not establish authority.|Both systems overwrite each other hourly|This can perpetuate conflicting states.
Where should monetary consistency be strongest?|Allocation, component balances and accounting event generation should share a controlled transaction boundary.|Allocation commits but journal generation is lost on process crash. Which design reduces this risk?|Atomic allocation and durable accounting event creation|The durable monetary state survives as one consistent result.|Notify by email before writing balances|A notification cannot make the ledger atomic.|Use only in-memory events|Memory is lost on crash.
What is safe to publish through an outbox?|Persist downstream work with the monetary transaction, then deliver and retry it separately.|A collection is posted but SMS delivery fails. What is the sensible recovery?|Retry the notification from durable outbox data|The posted money need not be reposted to retry a message.|Reverse the receipt solely because SMS failed|Notification failure does not undo cash or allocation.|Ask the borrower to pay again|It risks collecting twice.
Why is rail success not always final cash proof?|Rail response, settlement, bank statement and reconciliation can describe distinct stages.|A gateway reports success; settlement is due tomorrow under the stated process. What is still unresolved?|Bank settlement / reconciliation|The payment response does not prove the later settlement step.|Loan identity necessarily changed|Nothing in the scenario changes the loan identity.|The interest rate must reset|A gateway response does not change contract pricing.
What should a GL control account reconcile to?|The matching aggregate of account-level sub-ledger movements or balances, using aligned dimensions and cutoff.|GL principal is ₹9,00,000; matching sub-ledger principal is ₹8,95,000. What is the identified break?|₹5,000 unreconciled difference|The absolute difference is ₹5,000, requiring traced investigation.|₹17,95,000 difference|This adds the balances instead of comparing them.|No difference because both are assets|Shared account type does not make different amounts reconcile.
Why separate loan state from workflow state?|A pending partner payout or document task is not the same fact as a borrower debt balance.|Borrower dues were paid but the partner bank transfer failed. What remains pending?|Partner settlement workflow|Borrower allocation can remain intact while payout is retried.|The borrower EMI automatically becomes unpaid|Internal payout failure does not erase the received payment.|A new loan sanction|No new borrowing has occurred.
How should a boundary integration be evaluated?|Prove ownership, IDs, duplicate handling, error recovery, cutoff and reconciliation with actual scenarios.|A vendor says GL integration is simply a daily file. Which demonstration adds the most useful evidence?|Restart the failed batch and reconcile event-level control totals|It demonstrates integrity through failure and recovery.|Only change the dashboard color|Visual styling does not prove financial integrity.|Hide rejected rows|Rejected postings must remain visible as exceptions.
''')
module('math','Loan math and cashflow yield','PV, EMI, IRR, APR and EIR without conflation',[4,8,10,30,33],[
'For monthly reducing installments, r is the monthly rate, n the payment count and P the opening principal. EMI = P × r / (1 − (1+r)^−n), with P/n when r=0. Nominal annual rate divided by twelve is a convention for this model, rather than an assertion about every product.',
'Each scheduled row has interest = opening principal × r, principal = EMI − interest, closing = opening − principal. This model assumes installments are paid as scheduled. If they bounce, contractual scheduled closing and actual unpaid funded exposure can diverge.',
'Present value discounts future cashflows. IRR is a rate that makes net present value zero; the periodic IRR depends on cashflow spacing. Annual effective yield from monthly IRR m is (1+m)^12 − 1. Simply multiplying by twelve produces a nominal annualization.',
'APR is a borrower cost disclosure under the applicable method and cost inclusion rules. EIR is a financial accounting yield using relevant expected contractual flows and qualifying fees or transaction costs. A generic IRR is a mathematical tool used in such calculations, not a complete regulatory APR implementation.',
'Worked example: lend ₹1,00,000 now and receive ₹1,10,000 in exactly one year, with no other flows: IRR is 10%. If a ₹2,000 fee is withheld so the borrower receives ₹98,000, the same repayment implies 12.2449% one-year borrower cashflow yield. This illustrative yield is not automatically the required APR for every product.'
],'''
What is present value?|Future cash discounted to the valuation date using a stated periodic rate and timing.|₹1,10,000 is due exactly one year later. At a 10% annual discount rate, PV is what?|₹1,00,000|₹1,10,000 ÷ 1.10 = ₹1,00,000.|₹1,21,000|This compounds forward instead of discounting.|₹10,000|This is the difference, not the discounted value.
What is the monthly rate in this nominal model?|The stated nominal annual rate divided by twelve for monthly reducing calculations.|A model specifies 12% nominal annually divided by twelve. Monthly rate is what?|1%|12% ÷ 12 = 1% per month.|12%|That is the annual nominal label.|0.12%|The division is numerically incorrect.
How is the first EMI interest component calculated?|Opening funded principal multiplied by the specified periodic rate.|₹2,00,000 opens a month at 1% monthly interest. EMI is ₹8,000. Principal component is what?|₹6,000|Interest ₹2,000; principal ₹8,000 − ₹2,000 = ₹6,000.|₹8,000|It ignores the interest portion.|₹2,000|This is the interest component.
What does zero-rate EMI become?|With no fees or interest and equal installments, payment is principal divided by count.|₹1,20,000 is repaid in twelve equal monthly installments at zero interest, no fees. Payment is what?|₹10,000|₹1,20,000 ÷ 12 = ₹10,000.|₹12,000|This does not amortize the specified amount over twelve periods.|Undefined|Use the zero-rate limit rather than divide by zero in the general formula.
What does IRR solve?|The discount rate at which the signed dated cashflows have zero NPV.|Lender pays ₹50,000 today and receives ₹55,000 exactly one year later, no other flows. Annual IRR is what?|10%|−50,000 + 55,000 / 1.10 = 0.|5%|That discount rate leaves positive NPV.|110%|1.10 is the growth factor, not a 110% return.
Why do withheld fees change borrower cashflow yield?|The borrower receives less net cash but may owe the same future payments.|Borrower receives ₹98,000 after a fee and repays ₹1,10,000 in one year. Illustrative one-year yield rounded to 2 decimals is what?|12.24%|110,000 / 98,000 − 1 = 12.2449%.|10.00%|That uses ₹1,00,000 rather than net proceeds.|2.00%|The fee rate alone omits interest and timing.
How do nominal and effective annual rates differ?|Monthly compounding gives effective annual growth of (1+m)^12 − 1.|Monthly IRR is 1%. What is effective annual yield rounded to 2 decimals?|12.68%|(1.01)^12 − 1 is about 12.6825%.|12.00%|This is the simple nominal annualization.|1.00%|This is the monthly rate.
Why are APR and EIR separate measures?|Borrower disclosure cost and lender accounting yield can use different inclusion rules and purposes.|A finance team equates contractual rate, APR and EIR without testing fees. What is the central flaw?|They have different purposes and cashflow inclusion rules|Equality can occur, but cannot be assumed merely from the rate label.|EIR is always zero|EIR can be a positive accounting yield.|APR never considers fees|Fee inclusion is central to cost disclosure where required.
What is a limitation of an IRR solver?|Non-conventional cashflows can have multiple roots or no suitable unique IRR.|Cashflow signs switch from negative to positive to negative. What should an analyst check?|Whether IRR is unique and economically interpretable|Repeated sign changes can admit multiple IRRs.|Assume every solver output is the sole correct yield|A converged root does not prove uniqueness.|Ignore dates and signs|Both affect discounting and roots.
Why must rounding be specified?|Billing, display and internal precision boundaries can change paise residuals.|A loan engine rounds each monthly interest to paise. A report rounds only the final sum. What should reconciliation expect?|Possible paise differences that need a stated convention|Per-period and aggregate rounding can differ.|Always exact equality without a convention|Rounding stages are different.|Permission to discard unexplained rupees|Differences need transparent, bounded treatment.
''')
module('schedules','Schedules, tranches and actual exposure','Separate expected, billed, accrued and paid',[7,8,30,35],[
'An expected schedule describes contracted future installments. Billing records what became due. Accrual records interest earned under the stated basis. Actual movements record collections and principal repayment. A shadow schedule supports simulations without changing the active contract.',
'Broken-period interest covers an irregular interval, such as disbursement before the first regular billing cycle. State the interest-bearing principal, rate, start/end convention and day count. Actual/365 and Actual/360 can produce different charges for identical dates.',
'Tranche products compute interest on confirmed funded amounts. Floating resets, principal prepayments and approved restructuring create versioned prospective schedules. Preserve the old version and the triggering event to explain historical bills.',
'A moratorium changes timing; it does not by itself waive interest. Accrual, separate recovery or permitted capitalization depends on explicit terms and applicable rules. A holiday shift also needs a due-date and interest convention.',
'Worked example: ₹1,00,000 × 12% × 10/365 = ₹328.77, rounded to paise after the calculation. At Actual/360 the same assumed ten days gives ₹333.33. If a scheduled ₹6,000 principal installment bounces, actual POS does not fall merely because the table says it should.'
],'''
What is the expected schedule?|A contractual projection of future amounts and dates under stated assumptions.|An EMI table assumes every due is paid. Month 3 bounces. What does its closing principal still represent?|Scheduled closing under the payment assumption|It is a projection; actual principal needs actual principal repayments.|Proof of actual POS reduction|A bounced installment provides no principal repayment.|An approved write-off|No write-off event is described.
What distinguishes billing from accrual?|Billing creates payable dues; accrual tracks earned interest on its specified basis.|Interest is earned daily but billed monthly. Between bill dates, which view captures the earned amount?|Accrual view|Earned unbilled interest belongs in accrual tracking.|Only the future payment instruction|An instruction does not measure earned income.|Collateral register|Collateral records do not compute interest accrual.
Why use a shadow schedule?|Simulate proposed changes without overwriting the active schedule.|A borrower requests a foreclosure estimate and declines it. What should remain active?|The existing contractual schedule|A declined simulation should not mutate active terms.|The rejected quote's closed schedule|Declining a quote does not close the loan.|No historical schedule|The historical contract must remain auditable.
What inputs define broken-period interest?|Funded principal, rate, interval convention and day-count basis.|At 12% Actual/365, ₹1,00,000 accrues for exactly ten specified days. Rounded interest is what?|₹328.77|100,000 × 0.12 × 10 / 365 = ₹328.7671.|₹333.33|That uses Actual/360.|₹1,000.00|That assumes a full 1% monthly period.
How does Actual/360 differ here?|For the same amount, rate and actual days, denominator 360 produces more interest than 365.|₹1,00,000 at 12% for ten days uses Actual/360. Rounded interest is what?|₹333.33|100,000 × 0.12 × 10 / 360 = ₹333.3333.|₹328.77|That uses a 365 denominator.|₹3,333.33|That is ten times the correct charge.
How do tranches affect interest?|The specified interest base follows confirmed funded amounts, not the whole sanction.|₹10,00,000 sanctioned; ₹4,00,000 drawn. At 1% monthly on drawn principal, pre-EMI interest is what?|₹4,000|4,00,000 × 1% = ₹4,000.|₹10,000|That charges the full undisbursed sanction.|₹6,000|That uses the undrawn amount.
What should a rate reset preserve?|Prior schedule versions, effective date, event, reason and approved new prospective terms.|A reset applies from July. What should happen to settled April bills?|Preserve the historical bills and create a prospective version|The effective date is July, not April.|Recompute April silently at the new rate|That retroactively changes settled history.|Delete April payment events|Payment evidence should remain immutable.
How do EMI and tenor reduction differ?|At a lower POS, holding tenor lowers EMI; holding EMI generally shortens tenor under otherwise unchanged terms.|A valid prepayment reduces POS; rate and remaining tenor stay fixed. What should the recalculated EMI do?|Decrease|At fixed rate and count, EMI scales with principal.|Increase solely due to the principal reduction|That contradicts the stated unchanged pricing and tenor.|Remain equal by mathematical necessity|Equal EMI would instead generally shorten tenor.
Why test holiday shifts?|Due-date movement and interest treatment are separate product conventions.|A contract shifts payment to next working day without extra interest. What should the schedule engine do?|Shift the due date using that no-extra-interest convention|The stated convention controls both effects.|Always charge extra days|This contradicts the contract in the question.|Always backdate cash as already received|Moving a date does not create cash.
What proves actual POS after a bounce?|Confirmed funded principal minus actual principal repayments and approved principal reductions under the chosen ledger model.|Actual POS is ₹90,000 before a ₹5,000 scheduled principal installment that bounces, with no other events. POS after bounce is what?|₹90,000|No principal was repaid in the given facts.|₹85,000|This incorrectly uses scheduled principal reduction.|₹95,000|The bounce itself does not add new disbursement.
''')
module('accounting','Component balances and double entry','Explain every rupee without counting it twice',[9,10,11,12,35],[
'This module uses a simplified performing-loan lender ledger: POS includes all unpaid principal, including principal already billed. Interest and charges use separate receivables; unapplied customer cash is a liability. Institution-specific standards, EIR and impairment treatment require separate policy.',
'Assets increase by debit and decrease by credit. Liabilities and income increase by credit. A disbursement debits loan principal and credits bank. Earned performing-loan interest in this assumed accrual model debits interest receivable and credits interest income. Collecting already accrued interest clears the receivable; it does not recognize that income again.',
'Billing may reclassify principal from not-yet-due to due without changing total principal. Never add overdue principal to a POS that already includes it. Build totals from disjoint components; define gross principal, due principal, accrued/billed interest, charges, tax and unapplied credits explicitly.',
'Receipt in two stages: first debit bank and credit payment clearing. Then debit clearing and credit principal, interest receivable or advance liability as allocated. The second stage has no new bank debit. Direct collection templates and suspense templates are alternatives, not two cash receipts.',
'Worked example: POS ₹1,00,000 includes ₹10,000 overdue principal. Interest ₹2,000, charge ₹500 and assumed tax ₹90 are disjoint; customer advance is ₹1,000. Illustrative closure amount = 1,00,000 + 2,000 + 500 + 90 − 1,000 = ₹1,01,590. Adding ₹10,000 overdue principal again would overstate it.'
],'''
What increases a lender loan asset?|A debit increases an asset; confirmed disbursement normally debits principal and credits bank in this simplified model.|A lender disburses ₹40,000 under the module's ledger assumptions. Which journal is correct?|Dr loan principal ₹40,000; Cr bank ₹40,000|The funded asset increases and bank cash decreases.|Dr bank; Cr interest income|This describes cash income, not a loan payout.|Dr interest expense; Cr principal|It does not record the funded asset and bank movement.
What does accrual do in the stated performing-loan model?|It records earned interest receivable and income before cash collection.|₹1,000 interest is earned, not collected, under the simplified accrual policy. Correct entry?|Dr interest receivable; Cr interest income ₹1,000|The receivable and earned income increase; no cash moves.|Dr bank; Cr income|No bank credit is described.|Dr principal; Cr bank|No disbursement is described.
What happens when already accrued interest is collected?|Bank increases and the existing interest receivable clears; earned income is not recognized twice.|Previously accrued ₹1,000 interest is received directly, no clearing stage. Correct entry?|Dr bank; Cr interest receivable ₹1,000|The existing receivable is collected.|Dr bank; Cr interest income again|This duplicates income already recognized.|Dr interest receivable; Cr bank|That moves cash out and increases the receivable.
Can overdue principal be added to inclusive POS?|No. If POS already includes due principal, adding it again double counts that exposure.|POS ₹80,000 includes overdue principal ₹8,000. Separate interest is ₹2,000. With no other items, total principal plus interest is what?|₹82,000|Inclusive POS plus distinct interest = ₹82,000.|₹90,000|This adds overdue principal twice.|₹74,000|This incorrectly subtracts overdue principal from inclusive POS.
Why is principal billing a reclassification in this model?|Moving principal from not-yet-due to due changes presentation, not total funded exposure.|₹5,000 of inclusive POS ₹60,000 becomes billed principal, no cash. Total POS after billing?|₹60,000|Billing alone does not repay or newly disburse principal.|₹55,000|This treats billing as a collection.|₹65,000|This counts the billed slice as new principal.
What is clearing-stage allocation's cash impact?|Bank was debited at receipt; allocation debits clearing and credits the settled components.|Bank ₹12,000 was already posted to payment clearing. Allocation clears ₹2,000 interest and ₹10,000 principal. Which debit is used?|Dr payment clearing ₹12,000|It uses the existing receipt and avoids another bank debit.|Dr bank ₹12,000 again|This would duplicate cash.|Dr principal ₹12,000|That would increase exposure rather than clear it.
Why is unapplied customer cash a liability here?|The lender holds cash pending allocation or refund; it is not automatically earned income.|No dues exist; customer advance receipt ₹9,000 is held. What credit is appropriate in this model?|Borrower advance liability ₹9,000|The institution holds an obligation to apply or refund the cash.|Interest income ₹9,000|The scenario does not say the amount is earned interest.|New principal asset ₹9,000|Receipt increases cash, not a new funded loan.
What does an impairment allowance do?|It reduces net carrying amount separately from gross exposure under the stated allowance model.|Gross carrying ₹2,00,000 and allowance ₹12,000, no other adjustments. Net carrying amount?|₹1,88,000|200,000 − 12,000 = 188,000.|₹2,12,000|A contra-asset allowance is deducted.|₹12,000|That is the allowance alone.
Are balanced journals sufficient proof of correctness?|Balance is necessary but wrong accounts, duplication or wrong economic events can still balance.|A duplicated receipt entry has equal debits and credits. Which statement is true?|The journal can balance while cash is overstated|Double entry does not prevent a duplicate valid-looking event.|Balance proves the receipt happened twice|A ledger cannot create bank evidence.|No reconciliation is needed|Bank and source-event reconciliation remain necessary.
How should a posted error be reversed?|Add a controlled compensating journal linked to the original, then post any correct replacement.|A ₹7,000 principal allocation was posted to the wrong loan; cash is real and remains in bank. Best correction?|Reverse the wrong allocation and reallocate the same receipt with audit|This restores loan components while preserving the one cash receipt.|Delete the bank credit|The actual money still exists.|Debit bank again on the correct loan|That duplicates cash rather than correcting attribution.
''')
module('receipts','Receipts, suspense and advances','Cash received is only the beginning',[13,14,24,25,36,37],[
'A real bank receipt can be unidentified, identified but unallocated, allocated, or held as a borrower advance. Use distinct typed states and liabilities rather than one ambiguous suspense bucket. Receipts need source IDs, bank references, amount, value date and state history.',
'Under the examples here, advance EMI parks early money for future obligations. It does not immediately reduce POS or earn future interest. A valid principal prepayment is a different instruction: it reduces principal when applied and triggers the specified prospective schedule change.',
'Applying an advance later debits advance liability and credits the due components. Bank moved when the advance arrived, not when it is consumed. Excess after dues clear follows the stated instruction or approved default policy; never silently invent a principal prepayment.',
'Two independent bank receipts are two real cash events even if they relate to one EMI. A repeated notification for the same bank transaction is one receipt. This distinction is essential when NACH and UPI both succeed.',
'Worked example: due interest ₹2,000 and principal ₹8,000; two distinct ₹10,000 receipts arrive. Total cash is ₹20,000. After one ₹10,000 due allocation, ₹10,000 remains unapplied or refundable under policy. Interest income is not doubled simply because two payments were made.'
],'''
What is an unidentified receipt?|Bank cash is confirmed but no borrower/loan attribution is established.|Bank receives ₹6,000 with no usable loan reference. First ledger treatment in this example?|Dr bank; Cr unidentified payment suspense|It preserves real cash while attribution is unresolved.|Clear a random borrower's principal|No evidence identifies that loan.|Ignore the bank credit|That would break bank-to-ledger reconciliation.
What is identified but unallocated cash?|The borrower is known but application to components is pending or blocked.|A ₹4,000 receipt belongs to a known loan but allocation is blocked by an exception. Which state fits?|Identified, unallocated receipt|Attribution and allocation are separate stages.|No cash received|The prompt confirms the receipt.|Financially closed loan|A held receipt does not prove all balances cleared.
Why is advance EMI not immediate principal repayment?|Under advance policy, cash is held for later dues; instruction and contract determine application.|₹20,000 future EMI advance arrives with POS ₹1,00,000 and no immediate application. POS immediately becomes what?|₹1,00,000|The specified advance treatment leaves principal unchanged until due application.|₹80,000|That assumes principal prepayment contrary to the stated policy.|₹1,20,000|Advance receipt is not new lending.
How is advance consumption posted?|Debit advance liability and credit due components without another bank debit.|₹10,000 advance is applied later to ₹2,000 interest and ₹8,000 principal. Which debit?|Borrower advance liability ₹10,000|Cash was received earlier; the liability is now consumed.|Bank ₹10,000|That falsely records new cash.|Interest income ₹10,000|The advance is not a reversal of income.
What does an explicit principal prepayment change?|It reduces applied principal exposure and creates the stated revised prospective schedule.|Valid ₹15,000 principal prepayment is applied to POS ₹90,000 with no other movements. New POS?|₹75,000|90,000 − 15,000 = 75,000.|₹90,000|That would be unapplied advance treatment.|₹1,05,000|The payment reduces rather than increases principal.
How should unexplained excess be handled?|Preserve the excess in a typed unapplied/refund state pending the governing policy or instruction.|₹12,000 received, dues ₹10,000 cleared; policy parks unexplained excess. What is parked?|₹2,000|12,000 − 10,000 = 2,000.|₹12,000|That ignores the completed allocation.|₹10,000|That is the amount applied to dues.
How are duplicate webhook and duplicate payment different?|Repeated reference for one transaction is a retry; distinct settled bank credits are separate cash events.|One UTR appears in three identical webhook retries. How many economic bank receipts should be posted?|One|The repeated transaction identity represents one cash event.|Three|This confuses delivery count with transaction count.|Zero even after verified settlement|Verified cash needs one receipt record.
What if NACH and UPI both really settle?|Record both cash receipts; allocate according to obligations and park/refund the excess under policy.|One ₹8,000 EMI and two distinct verified ₹8,000 payments, no other dues. Residual after clearing EMI?|₹8,000|Cash ₹16,000 minus dues ₹8,000 leaves ₹8,000.|₹0|That would silently discard one actual payment.|₹16,000|That assumes no dues were cleared.
How is an approved excess refund recorded here?|Debit the borrower payable or unapplied liability and credit bank; link to source receipt and approval.|₹3,000 excess is already a borrower payable and is refunded. Correct simplified entry?|Dr borrower payable; Cr bank ₹3,000|The liability and cash both reduce.|Dr bank; Cr borrower payable|That records another receipt rather than a refund.|Dr principal; Cr income|That neither clears the liability nor records cash out.
Why age suspense balances?|Unresolved cash needs owners, reasons, aging and reconciliation; a balanced GL does not resolve attribution.|A suspense total reconciles to GL but contains credits unmatched for 90 days. Best operational next step?|Investigate aged items with source-bank references and accountable owners|Reconciliation of total does not cure unresolved customer allocation.|Declare all suspense as income|The amounts may still belong to borrowers.|Delete old records|That destroys the cash attribution trail.
''')
module('waterfalls','Waterfalls and allocation reasoning','Compare bucket-first and component-first results',[17,18,38],[
'A waterfall orders application of a finite payment. Specify due buckets, component priority, permitted amount, event type, asset state and effective version. No sequence is a universal RBI allocation rule; these examples are deliberately stated product policies.',
'Vertical allocation here means choose the oldest installment and clear its tax, charge, interest and principal before moving to the next. Horizontal allocation here means clear a component across all overdue buckets before proceeding to the next component.',
'The same payment can leave different oldest unpaid amounts under different policies. DPD follows the remaining relevant due amount under the defined delinquency rule; it does not automatically become zero after any cash receipt.',
'Allocation must conserve money: payment = applied + unapplied. Each line must be nonnegative and no greater than its component due. Reversal restores the original lines, not a newly chosen waterfall based on the current product version.',
'Worked example: bucket A = ₹100 charge + ₹900 interest + ₹3,000 principal; bucket B = ₹100 + ₹800 + ₹3,000. A ₹4,000 vertical payment closes A. Horizontal charge → interest → principal clears ₹200 charges and ₹1,700 interest, then ₹2,100 A principal; A still has ₹900 principal. Total dues are ₹7,900 and remaining dues ₹3,900 either way.'
],'''
What is vertical allocation in these lessons?|Choose oldest installment first, then its ordered components before the next installment.|A has interest ₹1,000, principal ₹4,000; B has interest ₹900, principal ₹4,100. Vertical interest→principal applies ₹5,000. Which result?|A fully clears; B untouched|Bucket A totals ₹5,000 and closes before B receives cash.|Both interest components clear first|That describes horizontal allocation.|B clears first|The specified policy chooses the oldest A first.
What is horizontal allocation in these lessons?|Clear the chosen component across buckets before the next component.|A interest ₹1,000 and B interest ₹900. Horizontal interest-first applies ₹1,500 oldest-first within interest. Result?|A interest clears; B interest receives ₹500|The first ₹1,000 clears A interest, then ₹500 goes to B interest.|A principal receives ₹500|The policy has not finished the interest component.|B interest clears and A gets ₹600|That contradicts the specified oldest-first order.
What is the allocation conservation equation?|Receipt equals allocated amount plus unapplied residual.|Payment is ₹9,000, allocated lines sum ₹7,500. Unapplied residual is what?|₹1,500|9,000 − 7,500 = 1,500.|₹16,500|That adds rather than reconciles receipt and allocation.|₹7,500|That is the allocated amount.
Why can waterfalls change DPD outcomes?|They leave different oldest unpaid due components; the specified DPD basis determines aging.|Oldest principal remains unpaid after charges and interest clear. DPD rule ages unpaid principal or interest. What happens?|DPD can remain positive|The relevant oldest principal due is still open.|Any partial payment always resets DPD|The rule depends on unpaid dues, not just cash arrival.|DPD becomes negative|Early money does not create negative delinquency.
Why store the waterfall version?|Reproduce the actual allocation using rules effective for that event.|A receipt used rule v2; current rule is v5. Which version explains the historical allocation?|v2|The event's recorded version explains its original treatment.|v5 automatically|Today's rule may allocate the same payment differently.|No version is needed if total cash matches|Cash conservation alone does not explain component application.
How does waiver change allocation capacity?|An approved waiver removes the corresponding due before later allocation.|A ₹500 charge was fully waived before payment. How much may a later receipt allocate to that charge?|₹0|No charge due remains in the stated facts.|₹500|This collects the waived amount again.|Any amount if cash is available|Allocation is capped by actual due components.
What is a hybrid waterfall?|An explicitly defined combination of component and bucket priorities, rather than a vague label.|A policy clears all tax first, then oldest installment interest/principal. How should it be configured?|Ordered explicit selectors for tax and then buckets|The actual sequence needs executable, versioned steps.|A label saying hybrid with no step definition|The label is insufficient to reproduce behavior.|Random order at allocation time|Random application cannot explain customer balances.
What must allocation reversal restore?|Original affected component amounts and linked accounting, with an audited reversal event.|Original allocation cleared ₹700 interest and ₹2,300 principal. Full allocation reversal with cash held restores what?|₹700 interest and ₹2,300 principal due|It reverses the original lines; cash can remain in clearing.|₹3,000 interest only|This invents a different component mix.|Only the schedule display|Accounting and component balances also need restoration.
How should a component allocation be capped?|Apply the smaller of cash available and eligible due, then continue or park residual.|Cash ₹600; a first-priority charge due ₹250, next interest due ₹500. Interest receives what?|₹350|Charge consumes ₹250; remaining ₹350 goes to interest.|₹500|Only ₹350 remains after the charge.|₹600|This ignores the charge priority and exceeds remaining cash.
Why should policy examples be labeled?|Commercial order, tax assumptions and aging rules vary; stated examples do not establish universal law.|An app example uses tax→charge→interest→principal. What can a learner conclude?|That result follows the stated illustrative waterfall|The example is a policy computation, not proof of a universal statutory order.|Every Indian lender must use this order|No governing rule establishes that claim here.|Order never matters|Partial payments can produce different component and aging results.
''')
module('closure','Prepayment, foreclosure and settlement','Build quotes from disjoint components',[15,16,35,37,38],[
'A principal prepayment reduces part of funded exposure while the loan remains active. Foreclosure settles the full contractual closure amount as of a specified date. Negotiated settlement accepts less than contractual rights with an approved concession; it needs a distinct loss/waiver and reporting analysis.',
'Use disjoint quote components. In this module POS includes due and not-yet-due principal; add separate earned interest and charges/tax only once, subtract eligible credits once. An overdue EMI total cannot simply be added to inclusive POS because its principal overlaps.',
'Residual interest depends on the stated interest basis, effective rate, dates and prior billed/accrued amounts. A quote has version and validity. It is usually a simulation unless the process separately bills earned amounts. Issuing a quote is not receiving money.',
'Current fee eligibility requires lender category, borrower/purpose, sanction or renewal date, rate type at prepayment and relevant amount thresholds from the applicable current source. This app does not grade unverified current prepayment/KFS/penal-charge thresholds. A dated guide alone is insufficient.',
'Worked example: inclusive POS ₹50,000; distinct interest ₹600; assumed permitted charge ₹200 and illustrative tax ₹36; eligible advance ₹1,000. Quote ₹49,836. A ₹49,000 receipt leaves ₹836 unpaid under an exact-payment policy. Financial closure remains pending; approved tolerances must be explicit rather than invented.'
],'''
How is part-prepayment different from foreclosure?|Part-prepayment reduces principal while the loan remains active; foreclosure clears the full contractual closure obligation.|A valid ₹20,000 principal prepayment is applied to a ₹1,00,000 loan without full closure. What is true?|Loan remains active with lower principal and the prescribed revised schedule|Only part of principal is paid.|No-dues certificate necessarily follows|There are remaining loan obligations.|The payment must be all interest|The instruction explicitly identifies principal prepayment.
What makes a quote reproducible?|As-of date, validity, balances, component formulas and schedule/rate/rule versions.|A customer challenges a quote six months later. Which evidence is strongest?|Archived quote inputs and effective rule versions|They permit reconstruction of that historical result.|Today's rates alone|The historical quote may have used different rates.|Only the total typed into a spreadsheet|A total without components does not explain the amount.
Does creating a shadow quote itself move cash?|No cash is received merely by calculating a quote.|A ₹75,000 shadow quote is generated with no billed new items or payment. Which cash journal is needed?|None for the quote alone|The question specifies simulation with no cash movement.|Dr bank ₹75,000|No receipt occurred.|Cr bank ₹75,000|No payout occurred.
How do you avoid principal overlap in a closure quote?|If POS already includes due principal, add only distinct interest, charges and eligible adjustments.|POS ₹1,00,000 includes overdue principal ₹10,000; overdue interest ₹2,000; no other items. Quote?|₹1,02,000|POS already contains the due principal; add only separate interest.|₹1,12,000|This counts the ₹10,000 principal slice twice.|₹92,000|It removes principal that is still owed.
How are eligible advances used in the example quote?|Subtract the eligible unapplied credit once or apply it once before quoting.|POS ₹50,000; separate interest ₹600; charge ₹200; assumed tax ₹36; eligible advance ₹1,000. Quote?|₹49,836|50,000 + 600 + 200 + 36 − 1,000 = 49,836.|₹50,836|This omits the advance credit.|₹48,836|This subtracts the advance twice.
What does an expired quote require?|Recompute or validate under a controlled process; preserve the old quote and receipt state.|A customer pays yesterday's expired quote; current amount needs recalculation. Best next action?|Hold/identify cash and validate a current quote before closure|Expired assumptions should not silently drive final closure.|Automatically close regardless of residual balances|The current obligation may differ.|Delete the expired quote|It remains useful audit evidence.
What is the result of short-paying an exact quote?|Under the stated exact-payment policy, residual dues remain and closure is pending.|Valid exact quote ₹49,836; receipt ₹49,000; no tolerance or waiver. Remaining due?|₹836|49,836 − 49,000 = 836.|₹0|No tolerance or concession was authorized.|₹49,836|This ignores the receipt.
What makes settlement distinct?|Approved acceptance of less than contractual dues requires concession accounting and appropriate status analysis.|Contractual dues ₹80,000; approved final settlement cash ₹65,000, no other credits. Concession is what?|₹15,000|80,000 − 65,000 = 15,000 rights waived or otherwise treated under policy.|₹65,000|This is cash accepted, not the concession.|₹0|Settlement for less than dues includes a concession in these facts.
How should foreclosure excess be treated?|Clear owed amounts and record remaining real cash as borrower payable/unapplied pending refund.|Closure obligation ₹30,000; customer pays ₹31,200, no other dues. Refundable excess under policy?|₹1,200|31,200 − 30,000 = 1,200.|₹31,200|The closure obligation has consumed ₹30,000.|₹0 with all excess as income|The example policy requires excess refund treatment.
What is needed before implementing a live charge rule?|Read the current applicable source and capture entity class, date, purpose, rate and other eligibility attributes.|A product manager wants one foreclosure fee for every Indian loan based only on POS. Best response?|Require current scoped eligibility and disclosure evidence before applying the fee|Amount alone does not establish permission or scope.|Apply the same percentage universally|This ignores entity and borrower distinctions.|Assume no borrower ever pays any charge|That is also an unsupported universal rule.
''')
module('delinquency','Delinquency, reversal and recovery','Track arrears separately from losses',[12,23,31,35,40,41],[
'DPD measures age of the oldest relevant unpaid obligation using the institution/product rule and local date cutoff. The exercises here define DPD as date difference from oldest unpaid principal or interest due; charges alone are excluded unless explicitly stated. This is an illustrative calculation, not a universal asset-classification rule.',
'A failed collection does not repay principal. A chargeback reverses a prior actual receipt/allocation through controlled events and can restore dues. DPD should then be recomputed from the restored due dates, rather than simply set to the transaction date.',
'Regulatory NPA, contractual delinquency and financial accounting impairment are related but distinct. Applicable entity, product and accounting standards determine recognition and provisioning. Do not convert an illustrative more-than-90-day scenario into every product\'s current statutory rule.',
'Under a stated allowance/write-off policy, provision expense increases an allowance; an approved write-off removes the specified asset against allowance or expense. An accounting write-off does not itself prove legal release of the borrower. Recovery needs a surviving rights trail and the applicable accounting policy.',
'Worked example: principal due ₹5,000 and interest due ₹1,000 on 1 June. On 11 June, both remain unpaid: defined DPD is ten days. If ₹1,000 clears only interest, principal remains aged from 1 June. If all relevant dues clear, defined DPD becomes zero. A later reversal restores the original due state for recalculation.'
],'''
What does DPD measure in these exercises?|Calendar-date age of the oldest unpaid principal or interest due, with stated cutoff and no charge-only aging.|Principal due 1 June remains unpaid on 11 June; date-difference convention is specified. DPD?|10 days|11 June minus 1 June is ten calendar days.|11 days|That incorrectly uses inclusive counting under the stated convention.|0 days|The relevant principal remains unpaid.
Does a failed payment reduce funded exposure?|No principal repayment occurs merely from a failed payment attempt.|An unpaid EMI's principal is ₹4,000; NACH returns failed with no bank credit. What principal collection is posted?|₹0|No receipt was received or allocated.|₹4,000|That would credit an unsuccessful collection.|The full sanctioned limit|Sanction is unrelated to this collection failure.
What if only interest clears under the defined DPD rule?|Unpaid aged principal still keeps the oldest relevant bucket open.|₹1,000 interest and ₹5,000 principal are overdue; ₹1,000 clears interest only. DPD outcome?|Still ages the unpaid principal|The defined relevant principal due remains open.|Automatically zero|A partial component payment does not fully cure the due.|Negative one day|Delinquency is not negative under this definition.
Are NPA and accounting credit impairment synonyms?|No. Test their applicable regulatory and accounting definitions separately.|An engineer maps every regulatory NPA flag directly to an Ind-AS stage with no separate rules. Main issue?|Different definitions and regimes need explicit mapping|Related labels cannot replace source-specific analysis.|Every NPA always has zero carrying amount|NPA classification does not necessarily remove the whole asset.|Accounting standards never consider credit risk|Impairment accounting does consider credit risk.
How does a chargeback affect allocation?|Reverse the relevant cash/allocation and restore affected dues through linked compensating events.|A verified ₹6,000 receipt is fully charged back after clearing ₹1,000 interest and ₹5,000 principal. What must be restored?|Original ₹1,000 interest and ₹5,000 principal due, with linked reversal|The original paid components must be reinstated under the stated reversal.|Only ₹6,000 new interest|That changes the economic component mix.|Nothing because it once showed paid|A reversed cash event needs correction.
What is memo interest under the illustrative policy?|Recovery tracking separate from recognized P&L income under that specified policy.|Policy requires off-P&L memo tracking of post-default interest. Which posting violates that policy?|Automatically crediting normal interest income|The stated policy excludes normal income recognition for that memo amount.|Recording a controlled memo record|This is the required separate tracking.|Keeping links to the overdue contract|Those links support recovery traceability.
What is an allowance in the stated model?|A contra-asset estimate booked separately from gross principal.|Policy books ₹8,000 impairment allowance against a ₹1,00,000 gross asset. Which statement is correct?|Gross asset stays ₹1,00,000; net is ₹92,000 before other adjustments|Allowance reduces net value without a principal repayment.|Borrower paid ₹8,000 cash|Provisioning is not collection.|The contract necessarily ends|An allowance does not itself close legal obligations.
Does accounting write-off always cancel legal recovery?|No; legal rights and borrower release need separate evidence and policy.|An approved accounting write-off occurs but contract rights remain enforceable in this hypothetical. What should LMS retain?|Off-book rights and recovery event trail|The prompt says rights survive.|A fabricated no-dues certificate|That would contradict surviving rights.|Only the deleted asset amount with no borrower link|Recovery needs identity and source-event references.
How is a post-write-off recovery recognized?|Use the applicable institution's policy; do not invent one universal recovery-income entry.|A post-write-off ₹5,000 bank recovery arrives but accounting policy is not supplied. What can be safely concluded?|Cash moved; recognition account requires the applicable policy|Bank receipt is known, but income/provision treatment needs policy.|Always credit ordinary EMI interest income|That is an unsupported universal accounting choice.|No receipt record is needed|Real bank cash must be tracked.
How do delinquency and collections workflow differ?|DPD is a calculated obligation state; contact attempts and promises are operational workflow facts.|An agent records a promise to pay next week without any receipt or waiver. Current overdue balance does what?|Remains unpaid and continues the stated aging|A promise is not a payment or concession.|Clears automatically|No obligation-changing event occurred.|Becomes new disbursement|A promise does not fund additional credit.
''')
module('colending','Co-lending shares and escrow','Principal ratio is not interest entitlement',[19,20,21,31,39],[
'Co-lending adds lender-share and settlement views to the borrower contract. For an illustrative two-lender loan, preserve borrower obligations, lender A principal/interest, lender B principal/interest, and escrow cash/settlement liabilities. Do not treat a partner report as a substitute for a share ledger.',
'In the assumed commercial example, A funds 80% at 10.5% and B funds 20% at 18%. Borrower blended rate is 12%. A month of interest on ₹1,00,000 is ₹1,000: A earns ₹700 and B ₹300. Principal repayment follows 80:20, but interest follows the share balance times each share rate.',
'If the receipt has ₹4,000 principal plus ₹1,000 interest, A entitlement is ₹3,200 + ₹700 = ₹3,900; B is ₹800 + ₹300 = ₹1,100. Splitting total cash 80:20 would produce ₹4,000 and ₹1,000 and misallocate interest under these assumptions.',
'Escrow receipt, allocation and payout are distinct. A settled borrower receipt can clear dues while a partner payout remains pending. Failed internal payout leaves a partner liability and exception; it must not automatically make the borrower pay twice.',
'Current CLA rules are entity-scoped. New in-scope arrangements governed by current Part B have verified retention, booking, escrow, share-account and classification controls. Legacy arrangements need transition analysis. The Sources screen records the current successors and held topics; the controls module tests only approved scoped claims.'
],'''
What four views make the example co-lending model useful?|Borrower obligations, both lender share ledgers, and escrow/settlement cash.|A vendor stores only borrower POS and an end-month 80:20 spreadsheet. What critical capability is missing?|Event-level share and settlement ledgers|A spreadsheet ratio cannot trace every principal, interest and payout event.|A second borrower loan application|A new application does not create accounting traceability.|A colorful graph|A graph does not substitute for ledgers.
How is the assumed blended rate computed?|Funding-share-weighted lender rates under the stated terms.|A funds 80% at 10.5%; B 20% at 18%. Borrower blended nominal rate?|12%|0.8 × 10.5% + 0.2 × 18% = 12%.|14.25%|That is an unweighted average.|10.5%|That ignores B's share and rate.
Why is interest share different from principal share?|Interest is share balance times lender rate; different rates change the entitlement proportions.|₹1,00,000 POS; A 80% at 10.5%, B 20% at 18%, annual/12 monthly model. A monthly interest?|₹700|80,000 × 10.5% ÷ 12 = 700.|₹800|That splits total ₹1,000 interest 80:20 incorrectly.|₹1,000|That is total borrower interest.
How much interest belongs to B in the example?|₹20,000 share principal at 18% annual/12 earns ₹300 per month.|Same ₹1,00,000 80:20 example with 10.5%/18% share rates. B interest as a percentage of total ₹1,000 interest?|30%|B interest ₹300 ÷ total ₹1,000 = 30%.|20%|That is B's principal proportion.|80%|That is A's principal proportion.
How is principal allocated under the stated 80:20 rule?|Split principal repayment by the agreed principal ratio and track rounding residual.|Borrower principal payment ₹4,000 is split 80:20. A principal entitlement?|₹3,200|4,000 × 80% = 3,200.|₹2,800|That applies the 70% interest proportion to principal.|₹4,000|That assigns all principal to A.
What is combined entitlement in the example?|Add each lender's principal share and its separately calculated interest share.|Receipt principal ₹4,000 and interest ₹1,000; A principal ₹3,200 and interest ₹700. A total?|₹3,900|3,200 + 700 = 3,900.|₹4,000|That splits total receipt by 80% and misallocates interest.|₹3,200|That omits interest entitlement.
What remains when partner payout fails?|The borrower allocation stays supported by real receipt; partner payable remains for retry and recon.|₹1,100 allocated to B is unpaid because the escrow transfer fails. What balance remains?|₹1,100 partner payable|Entitlement is outstanding until payout is confirmed.|₹1,100 new borrower overdue solely for this failure|Internal payout failure does not reverse borrower cash.|₹0 because an instruction was sent|An instruction is not settlement confirmation.
How should co-lending rounding conserve cash?|Round one allocated share and give the exact paise residual to the designated other share under policy.|Principal ₹100.01 at 80:20; A rounded to ₹80.01. B residual?|₹20.00|100.01 − 80.01 = 20.00.|₹20.01|That would create one excess paise.|₹19.99|That would lose one paise.
How should advances be shared?|Follow the stated agreement: hold borrower-level or share-level liabilities, with consistent future application.|Agreement holds all early EMI cash at borrower level until due date. What should happen before application?|Preserve full borrower advance and reconcile escrow|No immediate lender payout is specified.|Invent immediate 80:20 income|The agreement calls for holding; future interest is not automatically earned.|Reduce both lender principal balances immediately|That assumes principal prepayment rather than advance EMI.
What reconciles share principal to borrower POS?|Sum of lender principal shares must equal borrower inclusive POS under the shared model.|Borrower POS ₹90,000; A share ₹72,000; B share ₹17,500. Identified principal break?|₹500|72,000 + 17,500 = 89,500, short of 90,000 by 500.|₹0|The shares do not sum to the borrower POS.|₹17,500|That is B's share, not the difference.
''')
module('reliability','Reconciliation and reliable event processing','Retry and reverse without inventing cash',[24,25,28,29,36,38,40,41],[
'Reconcile each layer with aligned scope and dates: bank cash to receipts; receipt to allocation plus residual; allocations to component movements and accounting; sub-ledger to GL; lender entitlements to settled payouts plus outstanding payables. Totals alone can hide cross-loan errors.',
'An idempotency key identifies one economic event across delivery retries. Enforce uniqueness durably. Reusing the same key with a different amount or loan is a conflict requiring investigation, not permission to overwrite the original.',
'Use transactional outbox to persist downstream work alongside the authoritative transaction. Consumers need deduplication too: at-least-once delivery can repeat. Kafka or a queue is not by itself proof of ledger completeness.',
'A saga coordinates multi-step operations with durable states. Payout instruction, bank debit and confirmation need separate identities. Reversal adds linked compensating events. Allocation-only correction can leave cash untouched; chargeback or refund actually changes cash.',
'Worked example: customer paid gross ₹10,000, gateway settles ₹9,850 after ₹150 contractual merchant fee. If the borrower is entitled to gross credit under the stated policy, record bank ₹9,850 plus fee expense ₹150 against clearing ₹10,000. Giving only ₹9,850 borrower credit confuses merchant cost with borrower obligation.'
],'''
What is economic idempotency?|One monetary event is recorded once even when the same delivery is retried.|A verified UTR with identical payload arrives after process restart. Best result?|Return the existing result without a new receipt|Durable identity survives restart.|Post a second receipt because memory cleared|That is not durable deduplication.|Delete the first receipt and post again|It destroys history and can duplicate downstream effects.
What should happen on a conflicting duplicate key?|Detect and hold the conflict; do not mutate the original economic event silently.|Same transaction key first says ₹5,000, retry says ₹7,000. Correct handling?|Conflict exception preserving the first record|Different payload under one identity requires investigation.|Silently replace ₹5,000 with ₹7,000|That changes posted economic history.|Post both under the same key|That defeats identity integrity.
What does a transactional outbox solve?|It closes the gap between committed monetary state and lost downstream publication.|Loan allocation commits; process crashes before message publish. With a persisted outbox, what next?|Restart publisher and deliver pending event idempotently|The work survives the crash.|Collect the borrower payment again|Payment repetition is unnecessary and harmful.|Assume message already delivered|Commit does not prove publication.
Why must downstream consumers deduplicate?|Reliable at-least-once delivery can repeat already delivered events.|Outbox retries one allocation event twice to GL consumer. What must consumer enforce?|One accounting export effect per event identity|Producer reliability can cause repeated delivery.|Exactly two postings because two messages arrived|Delivery count is not economic event count.|No source references|References are needed to deduplicate and reconcile.
What distinguishes allocation reversal from cash reversal?|Wrong-loan reallocation changes attribution; refund or chargeback changes actual cash.|Receipt cash is valid, but allocated to loan A instead of B. What should a pure allocation correction do to bank cash?|Leave bank cash unchanged|The same real receipt is reversed from A and reapplied to B.|Debit bank again|This invents additional receipt cash.|Credit bank as refund automatically|No customer refund occurred.
How do gross receipt and net settlement differ?|Merchant fees can reconcile net bank cash to gross borrower credit under the stated policy.|Borrower paid ₹10,000; gateway retains ₹150 merchant fee; gross credit policy applies. Borrower due credit?|₹10,000|The merchant cost is separately accounted for.|₹9,850|That passes the specified merchant fee to the borrower incorrectly.|₹150|This is the fee, not the borrower payment.
What is the escrow conservation check?|Opening cash + receipts − confirmed cash payouts = closing cash, with other movements separately identified.|Escrow opens ₹2,000, receives ₹10,000 and confirms payouts ₹7,500; no other movements. Closing cash?|₹4,500|2,000 + 10,000 − 7,500 = 4,500.|₹12,000|That ignores payout.|₹500|That drops the opening balance.
How do settlement payable and cash reconcile?|Opening payable + allocated entitlement − confirmed settlement plus explicit reversals = closing payable.|B payable opens ₹1,000; new entitlement ₹3,000; confirmed payout ₹2,500. Closing payable?|₹1,500|1,000 + 3,000 − 2,500 = 1,500.|₹4,000|That ignores the payout.|₹500|That ignores opening payable.
What does a saga retain?|Durable step states, references, retry conditions and compensating actions across long workflows.|Foreclosure cash clears but mandate cancellation fails. What is the strongest design?|Persist the pending cancellation step and retry with evidence|The operational step remains visible without redoing the receipt.|Lose the task after UI refresh|A long workflow cannot rely on ephemeral UI state.|Recollect the closure amount|The payment has already cleared.
How should cutoff breaks be investigated?|Align value date, posting date, settlement date and batch scope before comparing totals.|Bank statement closes at midnight; GL batch uses a 6 pm cutoff. A difference appears. First check?|Date and cutoff alignment, then trace unmatched events|Different scopes can create a timing break.|Write off the entire difference immediately|No cause is established.|Hide the late receipts|They must remain traceable and roll forward.
''')
module('controls','Current Indian controls and source judgment','Scoped rules, evidence and effective dates',[22,23,26,27,39,40,42],[
'Rule checked: 4 October 2026. The 28 November 2025 RBI consolidation withdrew listed earlier instruments while preserving prior actions and consolidating obligations. Current study therefore uses entity-specific successor directions. The supplied June 2026 handbook remains an educational source, with dated citations and corrected examples.',
'For an in-scope CLA governed by current commercial-bank Part B, each participating commercial bank retains at least 10% of an individual loan; shares are booked without avoidable delay and no later than fifteen calendar days after disbursement. If the originating bank cannot transfer the partner exposure share within the deadline, the loan stays with the originating bank and a later transfer follows Part A. Scope and transition rules must be checked before applying these lessons to an actual entity.',
'Current bank CLA rules require bank-held escrow, share reporting to CIC, and coordinated SMA/NPA classification for default in the CLA exposure. Classification information is exchanged near-real-time, no later than end of next working day. NBFC CLA successors also require separate borrower share accounts and a funding-share-weighted borrower rate.',
'For an in-scope NBFC digital loan under current Credit Facilities Directions, outsourcing leaves regulatory responsibility with the NBFC. LSP control over loan fund flows, directly or indirectly, is prohibited. Routing exceptions do not permit LSP control. Routing has specified exceptions, including inter-lender co-lending and other enumerated cases; avoid absolute no-exception slogans.',
'Current prepayment, KFS and penal-charge successor details are held out of current-law scoring because their full texts were not accessible to the independent reviewer. General fee eligibility, disclosure versioning and no-double-counting lessons here are design principles, not certification of a live legal matrix. Sources lists the held texts and historical lineage.'
],'''
Why identify entity scope before applying a direction?|Bank and NBFC directions, entity definitions, excluded categories and transition can differ.|A learner copies an NBFC digital-lending answer to every bank product. What needs checking first?|Applicable entity and current source scope|A verified NBFC claim is not automatically a universal banking rule.|Only the website color|Visual appearance says nothing about regulatory scope.|Whether the loan has an EMI|Repayment shape alone does not establish entity applicability.
What minimum individual-loan retention applies in current in-scope bank Part B CLA?|Each participating commercial bank retains at least 10% of each individual loan; do not retroactively apply this to legacy arrangements.|For an in-scope CLA governed by current commercial-bank Part B, both lenders are commercial banks; bank A proposes 95% and bank B 5%. What is the retention issue?|B is below the 10% individual-loan minimum|Section 101 requires at least 10% for each participating bank in this scoped scenario.|No issue because the shares sum to 100%|Conservation does not satisfy the individual minimum.|Every CLA must be exactly 80:20|80:20 is an example rather than the sole permitted structure.
What is the current in-scope bank CLA booking deadline?|Book respective shares without avoidable delay, no later than fifteen calendar days after disbursement.|For an in-scope CLA governed by current commercial-bank Part B, latest booking deadline after the originating RE disburses to the borrower is what?|Without delay and no later than 15 calendar days from disbursement|Section 112 ties timely booking and the outer calendar-day limit to disbursement.|15 working days|The text uses calendar days.|Any time before annual audit|That ignores the specified deadline.
What happens if current bank CLA partner-share transfer fails within the deadline?|If the originating bank cannot transfer the partner exposure share within fifteen calendar days from disbursement, the loan remains with that bank; a subsequent eligible transfer follows Part A.|For an in-scope CLA governed by current commercial-bank Part B, the originating bank cannot transfer the partner exposure share within 15 calendar days from disbursement. What happens?|Loan remains with the originating bank; subsequent eligible transfer follows Part A|Section 114 applies when the partner exposure share cannot be transferred within fifteen calendar days from disbursement.|Backdate a fictitious partner booking|This conceals the missed control.|Delete the loan|The funded borrower exposure still exists.
Where do current in-scope bank CLA transactions route?|Escrow maintained with a bank, with contractual appropriation specified.|For an in-scope CLA governed by current commercial-bank Part B, required transaction route?|Bank-held escrow|Section 116 requires escrow routing in this scope.|An uncontrolled LSP wallet|It does not satisfy the stated route.|Any employee's personal bank account|Personal cash routing is not the required escrow.
Who reports a bank's CLA share to CIC?|The bank reports its own share under the current in-scope rule.|For an in-scope CLA governed by current commercial-bank Part B, who reports the bank's loan share to CIC?|The bank for its own share|Section 121 assigns reporting for the bank share.|Only the originator for all shares in every case|This does not meet the stated own-share responsibility.|No lender once escrow exists|Escrow does not replace credit reporting.
What is CLA classification exchange timing under current bank Part B?|Near-real-time exchange, no later than end of next working day, for CLA-default SMA/NPA alignment.|For an in-scope CLA governed by current commercial-bank Part B, one lender marks CLA-default NPA. Latest information exchange?|End of next working day, with near-real-time expected|Section 123 governs the scoped classification information flow.|End of next month|That exceeds the specified deadline.|Only after foreclosure|Classification exchange is not deferred until closure.
Does NBFC outsourcing transfer regulatory responsibility?|For an in-scope NBFC digital loan, the NBFC remains responsible for LSP acts and omissions under current section 6(7).|An in-scope NBFC digital-loan LSP breaches servicing controls. Is outsourcing itself a defense that removes NBFC regulatory responsibility?|No, the NBFC remains accountable|Current NBFC Credit Facilities section 6(7) preserves responsibility.|Yes, the LSP replaces the regulated lender|A service provider does not inherit all lender responsibility.|Only if the loan is co-lent|The stated outsourcing responsibility is not limited to co-lending.
Can an LSP control digital-loan fund flows directly or indirectly?|For an in-scope NBFC digital loan, no direct or indirect LSP control over fund flows; specified routing exceptions do not permit such control.|In an in-scope NBFC digital loan, an LSP wants direct or indirect control of loan fund flows. Correct answer under current section 10(3)?|Not permitted directly or indirectly; routing exceptions do not permit LSP control|Section 10(3) prohibits direct or indirect LSP control; payment-routing exceptions do not relax this prohibition.|Always permitted if a mobile app is used|App use does not authorize independent cash control.|All payment-routing exceptions are abolished|That overstates the rule and ignores enumerated exceptions.
Does DLG extinguish borrower debt or replenish after recovery?|For in-scope NBFC digital lending, DLG does not extinguish borrower liability; recoveries do not replenish invoked DLG.|Under current in-scope NBFC digital-lending section 25(4), a recovery arrives after DLG invocation. Does it replenish invoked DLG?|No|Section 25(4) prevents reinstatement from such recoveries.|Yes automatically|That contradicts the current scoped rule.|Only if the borrower uses UPI|The payment rail does not determine reinstatement.
''')
